  Context descriptor for the CARLOS application. The file name fixes the
  context path (/carlos); docBase points at the exploded webapp under
  /usr/share/carlos-emr, which is owned by root and read-only to the `carlos`
  uid the JVM runs as. Keeping the served tree outside the writable state
  directory is what stops a web-shell dropped through an application flaw from
  being served back: the process that could write it has nowhere to write it
  that Tomcat will execute.
-->
<!--
  suspendWrappedResponseAfterForward MUST stay "false" (Tomcat 11 flipped the
  default to "true").

  What the default does, precisely: when a forward returns,
  ApplicationDispatcher.doForward() unwraps the response-wrapper chain to the
  ResponseFacade and calls finish() on it. That is
  Response.setSuspended(true) -> OutputBuffer.setSuspended(true), and a
  suspended OutputBuffer silently DISCARDS every subsequent write and flush.

  The forwarded page's body does not reach that buffer during the forward; it
  sits in the filter stack's own buffers (javamelody wraps every request and
  turns all writer traffic into a PrintWriter over an OutputStream) and only
  drains during filter unwind, when each wrapper flushes its delegate. With
  the buffer already suspended, every one of those flushes is thrown away and
  the request ends with Content-Length: 0. Two verified user-visible failures
  on the packaged install (2026-08-30):
    - any JSP-to-JSP <jsp:forward> returned 200 with an EMPTY body;
    - a forward on a 4xx response came back as a raw 500, because
      ResponseSanitizationFilter's replay found the response already finished.
  Nothing is logged for either.

  So the fix is NOT that "false" restores a close that flushes something: the
  classic close the false branch performs is deliberately absorbed by the
  wrappers (PrivacyStatementAppendingFilter's DelegatingWriter has an empty
  flush() AND an empty close(), LogoutBroadcastFilter's degrades close to
  flush). The fix is that finish() is never called, so the buffer is never
  suspended and the unwind-time flushes actually reach the wire. Two
  consequences for anyone editing this stack: those no-op closes are load
  bearing, not dead code, and removing a wrapper's unwind flush would
  reintroduce the empty body. ResponseSanitizationFilter documents the same
  interaction from the filter side; keep the two in sync.
-->
<Context docBase="/usr/share/carlos-emr/webapp/carlos"
         reloadable="false"
         crossContext="false"
         useHttpOnly="true"
         sessionCookiePath="/carlos"
         suspendWrappedResponseAfterForward="false"
         privileged="false">

    <!--
      The application tree is ~9000 static files; the default 10 MB resource
      cache thrashes on it and shows up as slow first paint on every page.
    -->
    <Resources cachingAllowed="true" cacheMaxSize="102400" cacheTtl="60000"/>
</Context>
